{{/* Shared critical post-code review; neither mutates code nor advances a workflow. */}}
{{- $fix := eq .Kind "fix" -}}
{{- $title := "Implementation Review" -}}
{{- if $fix }}{{ $title = "Fix Review" }}{{ end -}}
This is a **code review only**, not permission to implement corrections. Be critical,
not a rubber stamp: labels, prior agent claims, and green tests alone do not prove correctness.

## Step 1 — Load LIVE state and identify the code under review

{{ template "beads-issues/shared/load-context" .Target }}

{{ if $fix -}}
Verify the live bead is a **bug** with `fixed` and without `support-question`.
{{ else -}}
Verify the live bead is a **feature** with at least one of `implemented`, `tested`, or
`verified`, and without `support-question`.
{{ end -}}
Both open and closed tickets are eligible; closure is not evidence of correctness.
If ineligible, stop without changes. If it is `in-flight` or has an active driver/claim,
stop rather than race ongoing work. Do not reopen a closed ticket.

Read the requirements, acceptance criteria, all relevant investigations, approved plans,
implementation/fix reports, testing reports, comments, parent/child/dependency context,
and later user clarifications. Separate authoritative requirements from agent assumptions.
Do not weaken requirements or invent obligations from inferred release/deployment gates.

Locate the actual changes from bead references to commits, PRs, branches, worktrees, or
uncommitted work. Inspect repository status, the relevant diff and base, then open the full
changed functions, callers, interfaces, and tests. Trace the actual end-to-end behavior.
Distinguish the submitted version from later changes and unrelated dirty work; do not
attribute the whole working tree or latest commit to this ticket. If the change set or
version cannot be identified reliably, report that blocker and ask for the missing reference
instead of guessing. Do not switch branches, reset, stash, commit, push, or overwrite work.

## Step 2 — Critically assess correctness and requirement coverage

- Map **each requirement and acceptance criterion** to concrete code paths and verification
  evidence. Mark complete, partial, missing, or contradicted behavior. Check the implementation,
  not merely whether it follows the plan: a flawed plan is not justification for flawed code.
{{ if $fix -}}
- Reconcile all investigation and reproduction evidence with the attempted fix. Does the
  changed code address the supported root cause, or only mask the symptom? Check every
  reported symptom, reproduction precondition, counterexample, and relevant environment.
  Challenge the diagnosis if the code contradicts it. A `fixed` label is not proof of a fix.
- Inspect the regression test: would it fail on the pre-fix behavior and pass with the fix
  for the right reason? Check it exercises the actual failing path, not a mock or assertion
  that bypasses the defect. Distinguish observed reproduction from prior unverified claims.
{{ else -}}
- Check the feature's user-visible behavior end to end, including integration, configuration,
  callers, persistence, and UI where relevant. Look for unconnected code, missing requirements,
  accidental scope changes, and tests that validate implementation details rather than behavior.
{{ end -}}
- Look for concrete logic errors, edge cases, incorrect invariants, error handling gaps,
  concurrency/lifecycle bugs, authorization and trust-boundary failures, data loss, compatibility
  and migration/rollback risks, and material performance regressions. Check downstream callers
  and surrounding behavior. Keep the review scoped; avoid speculative refactors and style churn.
- Assess architecture and maintainability where they affect correctness or lifecycle cost:
  ownership at the responsible layer, reuse of fitting existing mechanisms, unnecessary
  complexity, duplicated logic, and misleading documentation.
- Run the smallest sufficient safe checks using existing tests/build/lint commands when
  available. Do not edit repository files, add tests, run auto-fix tools, or perform destructive
  or externally mutating experiments. If a check requires writes beyond disposable build/test
  artifacts or unsafe side effects, propose it instead. Record exact commands, results, skipped
  checks and reasons; never imply a proposed check was run or a passing suite proves all behavior.

## Step 3 — Present the review and actionable solutions

Produce a concise **{{ $title }} [tier: Smartest]**:

- **Scope**: reviewed version/base, files and relevant paths; limitations or attribution gaps.
- **Verdict**: correct for the reviewed scope, changes required, or inconclusive; no blanket
  approval when requirements, code, or necessary evidence remain unavailable.
- **Requirements coverage**: requirement → code path → test/evidence, with explicit gaps.
- **Findings**, ordered by severity: exact file/line or symbol, triggering scenario, evidence,
  impact, and why it violates required behavior. Separate confirmed defects from hypotheses.
- **Proposed solutions** for each finding: the narrowest sound correction at the responsible
  layer, why it solves the problem, affected paths, compatibility/safety considerations, and
  a regression test or check that proves it. Show suggested code snippets/diffs in the report
  when useful, but do not apply them. Explain material alternatives and recommend one.
- **Verification**: commands actually run and outcomes, prior claims not independently checked,
  remaining risks, and the smallest next checks needed.

Explicitly say when no defects were found; do not manufacture findings or imply correctness
beyond the evidence. Preserve sound work. Proposed fixes and follow-up issues remain proposals.

